@page "/docs/common/render-modes"
@attribute [FollowsModeSwitch]

@* Render modes: the terms every page uses (static, interactive, circuit, IzzyBlazor's script, with
   JavaScript off), one example under its own mode switch, choosing a render mode, mode support on
   component pages, and state on static pages. From docs/nuget-readme.md, components.md, the
   [ModeSupport] metadata and Scripts/index.ts (the script Blazor loads on every page). *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="render-modes">
    <Lead>
        A render mode sets where a page's components run once the page has loaded. IzzyBlazor's components work in every
        render mode, and their HTML works with JavaScript off.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Terms used across the docs</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Every page in these docs uses these terms in the sense defined here.</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Static</strong> (static server-side rendering): the server renders the page once for each request
                and sends HTML. C# runs again only on the next request, such as a link the user follows or a form post.
                Pages in a Blazor Web App are static unless you give them a render mode.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Interactive</strong> (Server, WebAssembly or Auto): the page's components keep running after it
                loads, so <Code>@@bind</Code> and event handlers such as <Code>OnClick</Code> run C# when the user acts.
                Server runs them on the server, and WebAssembly runs them in the browser. Auto uses Server at first, while
                the WebAssembly runtime downloads, and WebAssembly once it's cached.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Circuit</strong>: what an interactive Server page runs over. It is the page's components and their
                state, kept in memory on the server that rendered the page, and a connection between that server and the
                browser. Static pages have no circuit.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>IzzyBlazor's script</strong>: a JavaScript module that Blazor loads on every page, static or
                interactive, so you add no script tag. It adds what HTML can't do alone, such as keyboard handling, ARIA
                roles, values that update as the user types, and checks before a form post.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>With JavaScript off</strong>: the page as HTML and CSS alone, with no script running. This happens
                when the user has turned JavaScript off, or when a script fails to load. Every page also starts as this
                HTML, so nothing is broken while its scripts load.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            An interactive page is prerendered: the server sends its HTML first, and C# takes over once the circuit or the
            WebAssembly runtime is ready. Blazor's own script loads on static pages too, and runs enhanced navigation and
            form posts.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">One component in each render mode</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Switch the render mode to see the same tabs on a static page and on an interactive page:</TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="tabs-modes" ModeSwitch />
        <TextBlock>
            The table shows what a few components do in each case. Each column adds to the one before it, so a static page
            also does everything in the first column.
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th></th><th>With JavaScript off</th><th>Static, with IzzyBlazor's script</th><th>Interactive</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Dialog</td><td>Opens from a trigger as a native modal, or from a <code>?dialog=id</code> link. Opened from a link, it shows but isn't modal, so focus can leave it.</td><td>A dialog the server opens becomes a real modal. After it closes, <code>?dialog=id</code> is removed from the URL.</td><td><code>@@bind-Open</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Table</td><td>Sorts, filters and pages through the URL</td><td>A select-all checkbox. Filters apply as the user types.</td><td>Sorting, paging, filters and selection bind to C#</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Tabs</td><td>Radio buttons and CSS switch the panels</td><td>ARIA tabs, with the arrow keys moving between them</td><td><code>@@bind-Selected</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Combobox</td><td>A native select that posts with the form</td><td>The user types to filter and moves through the options with the keyboard</td><td>Searches on the server as the user types</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>FileUpload</td><td>The file posts with a <code>Multipart</code> form, as an <code>IFormFile</code></td><td>The user can drop files on the zone. Type and size are checked before the post.</td><td><code>OnChange</code> streams <code>IBrowserFile</code>s</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>

        <Heading Level="2">Choose a render mode</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Keep a page static unless it needs C# to answer the user's clicks, and make only those pages interactive. You can
            set a render mode for each page, or for the whole app on <Code>Routes</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Render mode</th><th>Pick it for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Static</td><td>Most pages: lists, details, forms that save, settings. Sorting, filters and paging work through the URL, and forms work through a form post. Static pages need no circuit, so they scale and deploy like plain web pages.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Interactive Server</td><td>Pages that run C# on each change, such as an <code>OnClick</code> handler, a value bound with <code>Live</code>, or a search on the server as the user types. Each open page keeps a circuit on the server.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Interactive WebAssembly or Auto</td><td>The same kind of page, when its components should run in the browser instead of keeping a circuit on the server. These pages live in a <code>.Client</code> project.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>To make a page interactive, set its render mode under <Code>@@page</Code>:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@Interactive</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A circuit lives on the server that started it, so with several servers each user has to stay on one. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/interactive-stops-responding">Interactive parts stop responding in
            production</Anchor>. WebAssembly and Auto pages use <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code> in the <Code>.Client</Code>
            project; see <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/webassembly-and-auto">WebAssembly or Auto</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Render modes on component pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each component's Overview has a Mode support table. Where the component does less with JavaScript off, the
            table's "Without JavaScript" section says what changes. The API page marks the parameters that don't work in
            both render modes, such as <strong>Interactive only</strong> on C# events and <Code>@@bind</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">State on static pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A static page has no circuit, so C# runs only when the page is requested. The page keeps its state where the
            next request can read it:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><strong>In a form.</strong> A <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Form</Anchor> posts and validates on the server, and its fields bind to your model.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>In the URL.</strong> <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor>, <Code>FilterForm</Code> and <Code>Pagination</Code> keep sorting, filters and the page in the URL. <Code>Tabs</Code> with a <Code>QueryKey</Code> keeps its tab there, and a dialog opens from <Code>?dialog=id</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>In the element.</strong> Disclosures, menus, popovers and dialogs open and close in the browser. On interactive pages, <Code>@@bind-Open</Code> reports the change to C#, and setting <Code>Open</Code> opens or closes them.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><strong>In a cookie.</strong> The color scheme and the sidebar's state are kept in cookies. The server reads them, so each page arrives in the state the user left it in.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            Events such as a button's <Code>OnClick</Code> run only on interactive pages. On static pages, use a submit
            button in a form, or a link. <Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</Anchor> covers the usual
            surprises, such as <Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/state-between-requests">a value that resets on the next
            request</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>: one form for static and interactive pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">All components</Anchor>: what each one does in each render mode.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Interactive = """
        @page "/orders/{Id:int}/edit"
        @rendermode InteractiveServer
        """;
}
